WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.287
Welcome!

00:00:01.287 --> 00:00:04.445
In this Java tutorial, you'll use file paths to

00:00:04.445 --> 00:00:07.468
describe locations, write and read text using

00:00:07.468 --> 00:00:10.491
UTF-8, a rule for representing text as bytes,

00:00:10.491 --> 00:00:13.514
and append new text while preserving what was

00:00:13.514 --> 00:00:14.454
already saved.

00:00:15.101 --> 00:00:17.740
File paths, matching text encodings, and

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.643
deliberate write modes let applications save

00:00:20.643 --> 00:00:23.677
rosters, settings, or word dictionaries that a

00:00:23.677 --> 00:00:26.646
later run can recover after its variables are

00:00:26.646 --> 00:00:26.976
gone.

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:30.640
Our club coordinator starts a roster with Maya

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.282
and Luis, then adds Nora.

00:00:32.282 --> 00:00:35.303
We'll give this run its own practice directory

00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:38.390
and save one name per line, including a newline

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.769
after the final name.

00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:43.180
Success means reading the saved file back and

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.145
seeing all three names in order.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:47.662
We'll also inspect the recovered String's

00:00:47.662 --> 00:00:50.548
length, counting the stored newlines along with

00:00:50.548 --> 00:00:51.408
these letters.

00:00:51.408 --> 00:00:54.171
That count measures the String, not a general

00:00:54.171 --> 00:00:55.092
file byte size.

00:00:55.753 --> 00:00:58.370
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:58.978
Main.java.

00:01:02.746 --> 00:01:03.898
The file is open.

00:01:03.898 --> 00:01:06.813
We'll begin with the library types used for

00:01:06.813 --> 00:01:09.795
paths, file operations, encoding, the append

00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:12.100
option, and a checked I/O failure.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.150
These imports let the program use each type's

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:15.896
short name.

00:01:28.328 --> 00:01:30.219
Path represents a location.

00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:33.371
Files supplies operations that create, write,

00:01:33.371 --> 00:01:34.002
and read.

00:01:34.002 --> 00:01:37.294
StandardCharsets and StandardOpenOption provide

00:01:37.294 --> 00:01:40.586
named choices for those operations; IOException

00:01:40.586 --> 00:01:43.528
gives our handler a specific failure type.

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:46.494
Let's put the operations in the Main class and

00:01:46.494 --> 00:01:49.450
its main method, the entry point for this Java

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:50.221
application.

00:01:50.221 --> 00:01:53.112
A try block will keep the attempted file work

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:55.489
together before its matching handler.

00:02:02.077 --> 00:02:05.202
This structure holds the same operations as the

00:02:05.202 --> 00:02:05.801
notebook.

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:08.793
If an I/O operation fails, control will leave

00:02:08.793 --> 00:02:11.984
the remaining normal work and enter the handler.

00:02:11.984 --> 00:02:14.777
First, we need a separate location so this

00:02:14.777 --> 00:02:17.902
demonstration does not reuse an earlier roster.

00:02:29.671 --> 00:02:32.502
Creating the temporary directory actually makes

00:02:32.502 --> 00:02:34.731
a new directory and returns its path.

00:02:34.731 --> 00:02:37.441
Resolving roster dot text then builds a child

00:02:37.441 --> 00:02:40.332
path inside it; that second line does not create

00:02:40.332 --> 00:02:40.875
the file.

00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:43.585
The generated parent name can change on every

00:02:43.585 --> 00:02:44.368
complete run.

00:02:44.368 --> 00:02:46.898
Temporary also does not mean the directory

00:02:46.898 --> 00:02:48.946
disappears when this program ends.

00:02:48.964 --> 00:02:51.935
With the location ready, we'll store Maya and

00:02:51.935 --> 00:02:53.916
Luis as the starting contents.

00:02:53.916 --> 00:02:55.831
Each name includes a newline.

00:02:55.831 --> 00:02:58.736
We choose UTF-8 explicitly so the later read

00:02:58.736 --> 00:03:01.839
will use the same rule for recovering the text.

00:03:11.831 --> 00:03:14.508
This normal write creates the missing roster

00:03:14.508 --> 00:03:14.812
file.

00:03:14.812 --> 00:03:17.549
If the file already existed, the same kind of

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:20.104
write would replace its complete contents.

00:03:20.104 --> 00:03:22.659
It adds no line breaks beyond the newlines

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:24.059
supplied in the String.

00:03:24.059 --> 00:03:26.857
The path identifies where to write; the String

00:03:26.857 --> 00:03:28.256
supplies what to store.

00:03:28.278 --> 00:03:31.462
Nora arrives after the starting roster is saved.

00:03:31.462 --> 00:03:34.645
Replacing the file now would lose Maya and Luis,

00:03:34.645 --> 00:03:37.630
so the next write will select append mode and

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.553
add Nora at the existing end.

00:03:51.978 --> 00:03:54.739
The append option preserves the first two names

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:56.560
and adds Nora with her newline.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.145
This option alone expects the file to exist,

00:03:59.145 --> 00:04:01.495
which is why the normal first write came

00:04:01.495 --> 00:04:01.965
earlier.

00:04:01.965 --> 00:04:04.726
All three names now have their own line ending.

00:04:04.726 --> 00:04:07.428
To inspect what was actually saved, we'll read

00:04:07.428 --> 00:04:10.013
from the file instead of printing a separate

00:04:10.013 --> 00:04:10.953
expected roster.

00:04:27.494 --> 00:04:29.823
Reading with UTF-8 produces the recovered

00:04:29.823 --> 00:04:31.811
String, including its line endings.

00:04:31.811 --> 00:04:34.026
Print displays those stored characters.

00:04:34.026 --> 00:04:36.696
The following length report counts positions in

00:04:36.696 --> 00:04:37.377
that String.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:39.649
For these names, each letter and newline

00:04:39.649 --> 00:04:40.899
occupies one position.

00:04:40.899 --> 00:04:43.114
These whole-file helpers close the file

00:04:43.114 --> 00:04:45.783
resources they use; a much larger file may call

00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:48.169
for the line-by-line approach taught next.

00:04:48.198 --> 00:04:50.369
Let's finish the failure handler.

00:04:50.369 --> 00:04:53.461
It will report the actual exception message, so

00:04:53.461 --> 00:04:56.619
a failed operation cannot look like a successful

00:04:56.619 --> 00:04:58.198
read of an empty roster.

00:05:11.612 --> 00:05:14.461
The completed normal path creates a location,

00:05:14.461 --> 00:05:17.247
saves two names, appends one, and reads them

00:05:17.247 --> 00:05:17.563
back.

00:05:17.563 --> 00:05:20.476
Each of our three names has four letters; each

00:05:20.476 --> 00:05:22.248
also has one stored newline.

00:05:22.248 --> 00:05:25.034
Let's click Run and check both the recovered

00:05:25.034 --> 00:05:26.237
text and the count.

00:05:32.562 --> 00:05:35.231
The read-back shows Maya, Luis, and Nora in

00:05:35.231 --> 00:05:35.604
order.

00:05:35.604 --> 00:05:38.273
Twelve letters plus three newlines give the

00:05:38.273 --> 00:05:39.949
reported length of fifteen.

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:42.742
The final stored newline places Characters on

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:43.549
its own line.

00:05:43.549 --> 00:05:46.094
We see normal output instead of a failure

00:05:46.094 --> 00:05:49.074
report, and the recovered text shows that append

00:05:49.074 --> 00:05:50.812
preserved the earlier names.

00:05:50.866 --> 00:05:53.566
Now apply those same rules to a different

00:05:53.566 --> 00:05:54.027
roster.

00:05:54.027 --> 00:05:57.123
We'll change the first written text to Iris and

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:59.823
Bo, then change the appended name to Eli.

00:05:59.823 --> 00:06:02.787
The operations and newline rule will stay the

00:06:02.787 --> 00:06:03.116
same.

00:06:16.344 --> 00:06:19.045
Before running, predict every output line and

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:20.726
the number after Characters.

00:06:20.726 --> 00:06:23.306
Follow the first write, the append, and the

00:06:23.306 --> 00:06:25.167
stored newline after each name.

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:27.928
Pause here and work out the complete recovered

00:06:27.928 --> 00:06:30.569
String before you compare it with execution.

00:06:38.668 --> 00:06:41.739
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:06:41.739 --> 00:06:44.043
the actual saved-and-recovered text.

00:06:49.373 --> 00:06:52.051
Iris, Bo, and Eli appear on their separate

00:06:52.051 --> 00:06:54.473
lines, followed by a length of twelve.

00:06:54.473 --> 00:06:57.342
The names and their newlines contribute five,

00:06:57.342 --> 00:06:58.999
three, and four positions.

00:06:58.999 --> 00:07:02.059
This complete run created a new directory before

00:07:02.059 --> 00:07:05.119
writing, so it did not append Eli to the earlier

00:07:05.119 --> 00:07:05.948
run's roster.

00:07:06.002 --> 00:07:08.816
You used a path to identify a location, matching

00:07:08.816 --> 00:07:11.572
UTF-8 operations to store and recover text, and

00:07:11.572 --> 00:07:14.152
append to keep earlier contents while adding

00:07:14.152 --> 00:07:14.445
more.

00:07:14.445 --> 00:07:17.259
The observed results connect those operations to

00:07:17.259 --> 00:07:19.077
the coordinator's saved roster.

00:07:19.077 --> 00:07:21.422
In the notebook, you'll also compare the

00:07:21.422 --> 00:07:23.943
complete recovered String with the intended

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.702
text, including every newline.

00:07:25.772 --> 00:07:28.167
For a transfer question, imagine that the

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:30.854
coordinator needs to correct the entire roster

00:07:30.854 --> 00:07:32.607
rather than add a new arrival.

00:07:32.607 --> 00:07:35.411
Which write behavior should you choose, and what

00:07:35.411 --> 00:07:37.748
exact read-back test would show that the

00:07:37.748 --> 00:07:39.909
correction replaced the old contents?

00:07:39.909 --> 00:07:42.596
Use that distinction as you begin the notebook

00:07:42.596 --> 00:07:43.122
practice.
